+## What changed visually
 
-The earlier implementation sent all three curves through the centre panel. That correctly showed the relationship, but it obstructed chart metadata.
+The previous inner-rail kit was logically correct but the routes were still too prominent. This kit makes the relationship read like the reference:
 
-This kit adds an isolated routing engine:
+- the selected palace carries the strongest crimson treatment;
+- all three destination palaces use the same restrained related-palace treatment;
+- route lines use a source-to-target opacity gradient;
+- the route is almost invisible near the source;
+- it becomes only slightly more visible near the destination;
+- each route ends with a tiny crimson arrowhead on the destination border;
+- no target is visually presented as a different arrow type;
+- the protected centre information card remains above the routes.
 
-```text
-assets/relationship-routing.js
-```
-
-Each arrow now:
-
-1. starts at a separate anchor on the selected palace's centre-facing border;
-2. enters one of three dedicated inner-perimeter lanes;
-3. follows the shortest clockwise or counter-clockwise route around the centre;
-4. uses rounded SVG corners;
-5. leaves the rail close to the target;
-6. terminates at the target palace border with a small arrowhead.
-
-The centre panel is now a transparent shell with an opaque inset information card. The paths remain visible in the surrounding corridor but pass behind the protected reading area.
-
-## Visual hierarchy
-
-- selected palace: strong crimson highlight;
-- two trines: restrained gold highlight;
